    <rdfs:label rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Observation of Propagation Phenomenon Occurrence</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="N52ce2ff22a3942a0a09f4b4d93c58340"/>
    <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">As a propagation phenomenon is still a general phenomenon ("
            Smith, David Woodruff. « Phenomenology ». The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, édité par Edward N. Zalta, Summer 2018, Metaphysics Research Lab, Stanford University, 2018. Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2018/entries/phenomenology/.
            "), different points of view or observations may result in different experiences. A phen:Observation is a gufo:Perdurant that represents a spatial-temporal perspective of a phen:PhenomenonOccurrence (i.e., two observations from different people may differ in the number of details one can give).

            In practice, a phen:Observation may be used to identify what gufo:Individual are participating in a phen:PhenomenonOccurrence for a given node of the network for a given time interval.

            As phen:Observation designs a spatial-temporal cluster itself, it can be used to track valid spatial-temporal paths between several pairs of phen:Observation.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
  </rdf:Description>
